is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: aswa1, aswa2, aswa3, aswb1, aswb2, aswb3
diagnosis_text:
The campus “freezes” are caused by repeated Layer‑2 Spanning Tree topology changes, not by core routing failure. All access/distribution bridges are running classic 802.1D STP, and the access switches are treating end‑host ports as STP participating ports. Evidence: on multiple switches the kernel logs show continuous “topology change detected, sending tcn bpdu” and “received tcn bpdu” storms (e.g., aswa1 at 20:52:08–20:52:12 shows many ports repeatedly “entered forwarding state” and TCNs being sent/received). The distribution pair on side A (dswa1 root, dswa2 root‑port on bond0) and side B (dswb1 root, dswb2 root‑port) are stable per brctl show, but the frequent TCNs propagate from the access layer up to the roots and across each VLAN’s tree, flushing MAC/FDB tables, which briefly black‑holes/cycles unicast traffic. Core OSPF adjacencies across csw1/csw2 are healthy; the “freeze” impression matches L2 FDB flushes, not routed link drops.

Localization: On access switches aswa1/2/3 and aswb1/2/3, br0 includes client‑facing interfaces (eth0) and dual uplinks (eth2/eth3). Because client ports are not configured as edge/portfast, any benign host link flap or MAC move triggers a TCN that ripples to dswa1/dswb1 (the STP roots), seen by sustained TCN logs on both distribution and core devices.

Root cause: Access switch edge ports are incorrectly participating in STP, causing continual topology change notifications and FDB flushes.

Corrective fix: On all access switches, mark all true end‑host ports as STP edge/portfast and enable BPDU‑guard; keep STP only on uplinks and inter‑switch links (bond0 and uplink ports). If staying with Linux bridge, disable STP per‑port for single‑homed client ports or set them to edge; optionally migrate to RSTP to reduce convergence impact. This will stop TCN storms and eliminate the periodic freezes. 
=== END ===